================================================================================
NEOWAY N706B OPENCPU ENVIRONMENT & BUILD GUIDE
================================================================================

This document details the build scripts, compiler databases, and IDE tools 
established for the Neoway N706B OpenCPU project environment.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE BUILD SCRIPTS (build_<ProjectName>.bat)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Each project folder contains a dedicated Windows batch script (e.g., build_Blinky.bat).
When run, the script performs the following tasks:

1. Environment Definitions:
   - Configures the SDK root path:
     SDK_ROOT = E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009_SDK\N706B-A07-STD-OE_CN1X_ITRI-009_SDK
   - Automatically detects the current project folder:
     CUST_PROJ_DIR = E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\Code\<ProjectName>

2. Environment Bootstrapping:
   - Calls the SDK environment setup script:
     call tools\env_init.bat

3. Build Cleanup (Cache avoidance):
   - Deletes the existing SDK build directory:
     rd /s /q "%SDK_ROOT%\out"
     This is critical to prevent stale CMake paths and compiler caches.

4. CMake Configuration with IntelliSense Export:
   - Configures the project with Ninja build system generator.
   - Passes CUST_PROJ_DIR to the SDK's CMakeLists.txt to compile the custom project.
   - Sets -DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON to generate a compiler database.

5. Ninja Compilation:
   - Compiles the source files using the ARM GNU Toolchain.
   - Generates the target raw binary in:
     %SDK_ROOT%\out\bin\nwy_open_app.bin

6. Aboot Packaging:
   - Runs the SDK arelease.exe tool to package the compiled binary, standard bootloaders, 
     partition table, and cellular data modules into a single flashable zip archive.
   - Outputs the final package in the project's release folder:
     %CUST_PROJ_DIR%\release\nwy_open_app.zip

7. Copying the compilation database:
   - Copies the compile_commands.json to the workspace root:
     E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\Code\compile_commands.json

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. LANGUAGE SERVER INTEGRATION (compile_commands.json)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
In modern C/C++ editors (like VS Code/Antigravity IDE), code intelligence features 
such as autocomplete, diagnostics, "Go to Definition", and "Go to Declaration" 
rely on a Language Server (specifically, clangd).

Because the custom code is compiled against the external Neoway SDK, clangd 
cannot automatically find the header files (e.g., "nwy_gpio_api.h") without help.

How compile_commands.json solves this:
- The compile_commands.json file tells clangd exactly how every file in the 
  workspace is compiled, listing the include paths (-I) and macro definitions (-D).
- By copying compile_commands.json to E:\projects\DevelopmentLevelCode\Neoway_N706B\Code\, 
  clangd can read it and index all OpenCPU APIs.

Forcing Clangd to reload the database:
If "Go to Definition" or autocompletion stops working:
1. Open the Command Palette in your editor (Ctrl + Shift + P).
2. Run the command: "Clangd: Restart language server".
3. Wait for clangd to index the files. Symbol navigation will be fully restored.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. GIT & PROJECT RULES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
To ensure smooth operations, the following rules have been set up:

1. Windows Path Limit (MAX_PATH):
   The SDK contains very deep directory paths that exceed Windows' default 260-character 
   limit. To prevent Git from failing, run this command once:
   git config core.longpaths true

2. Tracking Large Assets:
   Git LFS (Large File Storage) has been configured in the SDK repository to track 
   large files (zip, rar, dll, exe) to prevent repository bloat and transfer timeouts.
